<article class="env-corollary" id="02NF">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02NF">Corollary <span data-tag="02NF">7.2.1.16</span>.</a> Suppose we are given a categorical pushout diagram of simplicial sets </p>
<div class="equation" id="02NG">
<span class="equation-label"><a data-tag="02NG" href="/tag/02NG">7.15</a></span>
<div>\begin{equation}  \begin{gathered}\label{equation:categorical-pushout-cofinal} \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \ar [r]^-{f} \ar [d] &amp;  Z \ar [d] \\ X' \ar [r]^-{f'} &amp;  Z'. } \end{gathered} \end{equation}</div>
</div>
<p> If $f$ is left cofinal, then $f'$ is also left cofinal. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      By virtue of Corollary <a data-tag="02NE" href="/tag/02NE">7.2.1.15</a>, we may assume that $f$ factors as a composition $X \xrightarrow {g} Y \xrightarrow {h} Z$, where $g$ is left anodyne and $h$ is a trivial Kan fibration. Setting $Y' = Y {\coprod }_{X} X'$, we can expand (<a data-tag="02NG" href="/tag/02NG">7.15</a>) to a commutative diagram 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ X \ar [r]^-{g} \ar [d] &amp;  Y \ar [r]^-{h} \ar [d] &amp;  Z \ar [d] \\ X' \ar [r]^-{g'} &amp;  Y' \ar [r]^-{h'} &amp;  Z'. } \]
</div>
<p>
       Note that the square on the left is a pushout diagram in which the horizontal maps are monomorphisms, and therefore a categorical pushout diagram (Example <a data-tag="01FJ" href="/tag/01FJ">4.5.5.12</a>). Applying Proposition <a data-tag="01FA" href="/tag/01FA">4.5.5.8</a>, we deduce that the square on the right is also a categorical pushout diagram. Since $h$ is a categorical equivalence (Proposition <a data-tag="01EG" href="/tag/01EG">4.5.4.11</a>), it follows that $h'$ is also a categorical equivalence (Proposition <a data-tag="01FC" href="/tag/01FC">4.5.5.10</a>). In particular, $h'$ is left cofinal (Corollary <a data-tag="02NC" href="/tag/02NC">7.2.1.13</a>). The morphism $g'$ is left anodyne (since it is a pushout of $g$), and is therefore also left cofinal (Proposition <a data-tag="02N3" href="/tag/02N3">7.2.1.3</a>). Applying Proposition <a data-tag="02N6" href="/tag/02N6">7.2.1.6</a>, we deduce that $f' = h' \circ g'$ is also left cofinal. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
